course: dictionary
lesson: 6496
title: Neatly: tidy and skilful
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Neatly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done. Most often it means in a tidy, careful way, with everything in order. «Write neatly» means your letters are clear and sit in line, and «fold your clothes neatly» means they are folded flat and tidy.
explain: Neatly can also mean cleverly and skilfully, with no fuss. «Tom the cat falls off the roof and always lands neatly on his feet.» Tom is not messy or clumsy when he lands. He lands easily and well, so neatly means skilfully here.
explain: Neat work helps in maths too. When you set out your working neatly, with digits lined up in columns, you and your teacher can follow each step and spot mistakes. To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence: «He copied his work tidily into the book» still makes sense, so neatly means tidily here.
fact: Neatly means in a tidy, careful way, with everything in order.
fact: Neatly can also mean cleverly and skilfully, with no fuss.
fact: Neatly is an adverb, and its comparative is more neatly.
fact: Useful phrases: «write neatly», «neatly folded», «neatly dressed», «fit neatly».
fact: The opposite of neatly is messily: «Her bag was packed messily» means it was in a heap, not in order.
exercise: «Please write neatly so your teacher can read every answer.» What does neatly mean here? | Very loudly | Very fast and messy | In a rush, with no thought | *In a tidy, careful way
exercise: «Tom the cat falls off the roof and always lands neatly on his feet.» What does neatly mean here? | Messily, all in a heap | *Cleverly and skilfully, with no fuss | Sadly and slowly | Crossly and loudly
exercise: Which word in «He copied his work neatly into the book» tells you how he copied? | into | book | *neatly | work
test:
«The bags were packed neatly and the beds were made.» What does neatly tell us about how the bags were packed? | Carelessly, in a heap | *With everything tidy and in order | Very quickly, in a rush | Noisily
Why should you set out your maths working neatly? | To make the page look messy on purpose | *So you and your teacher can see each step and spot mistakes | To use up more paper | Because neat writing is a sign of a bad mark
exam:
«A smooth shiny surface reflects light neatly, so we see a clear picture in it.» What does neatly mean here? | Messily, so the picture is blurred | Slowly, with a long pause | *Cleanly and skilfully, with no fuss | Very dimly, in the dark
